{
  "heading": "Step 3: Add a controller that returns a model",
  "axis": "language",
  "body": "Yon is the server part of Tachyon. A controller is a class in `server/controllers` that answers HTTP requests. `RequestRoute(\"/api/greeting\")` gives its path. `Public` says that anyone may call it: every route must say who may call it, or the build stops (see Who may call a route). `GetRoute` makes the method `getGreeting` answer GET requests on that path. Each language writes the name in its own style, such as `get_greeting` in Python or `GetGreeting` in C#. A Rust controller also has `fn new()`, because Tachyon makes the controller with it. A C++ controller is a header-only class in `GreetingController.hpp` that writes the markers as attributes, such as `class [[RequestRoute(\"/api/greeting\")]] [[Public]] GreetingController`.\n\nA model is a data type that describes the shape of a value. Here the model `Greeting` has one text field, `message`. The model lives in `server/models`, in a file named after it, such as `Greeting.ts`, `Greeting.py` or `Greeting.hpp`; a C++ controller includes its model with `#include \"../models/Greeting.hpp\"`. JavaScript reads the TypeScript model with `/** @import { Greeting } from '/models/Greeting.ts' */`. The method returns a Greeting, and Yon turns it into JSON. The same `ty preview` serves the pages and the controller on one port.\n\nResult: open /api/greeting. It shows {\"message\":\"Hello from Yon\"}.",
